Chapter 1 – The Problem Statement

1.1 The Latency Wall

The Earth‑Moon round‑trip signal delay is ≈2.6 s under ideal conditions—not an inconvenience, but an epistemological fracture. On Earth, queries to PubMed, geotechnical databases, or agricultural extension services resolve in milliseconds; the planet’s knowledge infrastructure functions as an extension of working memory. On the Moon, the same query takes 2.6 s at best, and during solar storms or the 14‑day lunar night may be impossible for hours, days, or indefinitely. A lunar colony cannot treat Earth’s internet as working memory. It must build its own.

1.2 The Decay Problem

Digital storage is not eternal. NAND flash leaks charge, magnetic media suffers domain drift, optical discs face substrate degradation, SSDs have finite program‑erase cycles. Lunar stressors—radiation (galactic cosmic rays, solar particle events), temperature cycling (−173 °C to +127 °C), micrometeorite impact, electrostatic charging—accelerate decay dramatically. Naïve redundancy without verification is merely decay in parallel.

1.3 The Coherence Problem — Five Named Failure Modes

The colony’s knowledge base is a living system. Mismanagement yields five distinct failure modes:

