Chapter 2 – Mathematical Foundations and Architecture

2.1 Knowledge State Space and Fidelity Metrics

Let \mathcal{K} be a measurable space of knowledge items. An item k \in \mathcal{K} is a tuple (c, \tau, \sigma, \pi):

· c: content.
· \tau: type (foundational, operational, ephemeral, tacit).
· \sigma \in [0,1]: semantic integrity score.
· \pi: provenance metadata.

The global knowledge state at time t is a probability measure \mu_t on \mathcal{K}.

Definition 2.1 (Semantic Fidelity). For a transformation T: \mathcal{K} \to \mathcal{K},

\Phi(T, \mu) = 1 - \inf_{f \in \mathcal{F}} \mathbb{E}_{k \sim \mu}\big[ \ell( f(k), f(T(k)) ) \big],

where \mathcal{F} is a class of semantic probe functions and \ell is a bounded loss. \Phi = 1 indicates perfect meaning preservation.

2.2 Layered Architecture as a Category

The six memory layers—Physical Storage (L_1), Knowledge Representation (L_2), Tactical (L_3), Operational (L_4), Institutional (L_5), Civilisational (L_6)—form a poset category \mathbf{L}.

Definition 2.2. A morphism f_{i,j}: L_i \to L_j (i \le j) is an interpretation map preserving fidelity. Compositions satisfy

\Phi(f_{i,k}, \mu) \ge \prod_{m=i}^{k-1} \Phi(f_{m,m+1}, \mu_m).

Theorem 2.2 (Fidelity Budget). If each adjacent map satisfies \Phi(f_{m,m+1}) \ge 1 - \varepsilon, then

\Phi(f_{1,6}) \ge 1 - 6\varepsilon + O(\varepsilon^2).

Proof. Union bound over independent loss events; see Appendix A.

2.3 Degradation and Regeneration Dynamics

Let F_i(t) be the fidelity of layer i, with intrinsic decay rate \lambda_i and regeneration rate v_i.

Proposition 2.3 (First‑Order Fidelity Dynamics).

\frac{dF_i}{dt} = -\lambda_i F_i + v_i (1 - F_i).

Steady state: F_i^* = v_i/(\lambda_i + v_i). Two derived quantities:

· Civilizational Memory Half‑Life: \mathrm{CMH}_i = \ln 2 / \lambda_i.
· Memory Resilience Index: \mathrm{MRI}_i = v_i / \lambda_i.

2.4 The Memory Legibility Trilemma — Formalised

Conjecture 2.4 (Memory Legibility Trilemma). For a knowledge domain with entropy H > H_c, let Q_{AI}(R), Q_H(R), and C_{FT}(R) be machine‑queryability, human legibility, and fault‑tolerance overhead of representation R. Then there is no single R such that simultaneously Q_{AI}(R) \ge \tau_{AI}, Q_H(R) \ge \tau_H, and C_{FT}(R) \le \tau_C.

Justification. The trilemma is rooted in source–channel separation: human narrative redundancy and machine‑oriented compression are in direct opposition, and fault‑tolerance codes add further redundancy. The classical separation theorem fails when the human cognitive channel is part of the system. Any representation claiming to meet all three thresholds must hide a translation layer T whose cost is omitted from C_{FT}.

2.5 Optimal Verification Scheduling — Water‑Filling Theorem

Each knowledge item k has criticality \kappa(k), corruption probability p_{\text{corr}}(k), verification energy e_{\text{ver}}, and fidelity gain \Delta\Phi(k) if repaired.

Theorem 2.5 (Water‑Filling Policy). The optimal schedule under a total energy budget E_{\max} satisfies

\text{verify } k \iff \kappa(k) \cdot p_{\text{corr}}(k) \cdot \Delta\Phi(k) \ge \lambda^*,

where \lambda^* is the water‑level chosen so that total energy equals E_{\max}.

Proof. The problem is a convex resource allocation:

\max_{\{x_k \in \{0,1\}\}} \sum_k \kappa(k)\, p_{\text{corr}}(k)\, \Delta\Phi(k)\, x_k \quad \text{s.t. } \sum_k e_{\text{ver}} x_k \le E_{\max}.

Relaxing to continuous x_k \in [0,1], the Lagrangian is

\mathcal{L} = \sum_k v_k x_k - \lambda \left( \sum_k e_{\text{ver}} x_k - E_{\max} \right),

with v_k = \kappa(k) p_{\text{corr}}(k) \Delta\Phi(k). The KKT conditions yield x_k = 1 if v_k/e_{\text{ver}} \ge \lambda, else 0. As e_{\text{ver}} is constant, the condition reduces to v_k \ge \lambda e_{\text{ver}} \equiv \lambda^*. ∎

Future experimental plan: Implement this scheduler in a trace‑driven simulator using analogue habitat data and measured radiation‑error traces, comparing fidelity against round‑robin and criticality‑only baselines.

2.6 Self‑Repair and Confidence‑Gated Escalation

With m redundant copies failing independently with probability p, majority‑vote recovery probability is

P_{\text{repair}} = \sum_{j=\lceil m/2\rceil}^{m} \binom{m}{j} (1-p)^j p^{m-j}.

If no intact copy exists, inferential repair solves

\hat{k} = \arg\max_{x \in \mathcal{K}} P(x \mid \mathcal{C}, \text{partial observations}),

and escalates to a human expert if the maximum posterior falls below threshold \theta_c.

Lemma 2.6 (Error Bound). The probability of silent erroneous repair is bounded by 1 - \theta_c.

2.7 Self‑Evolution as a Wasserstein Gradient Flow — Lyapunov Stability Proof

We now formally establish that the memory pruning process converges to a stable, minimal survivable knowledge core. We lift the dynamics into the space of probability measures using the 2‑Wasserstein metric from optimal transport theory, proving that semantic pruning follows the path of least thermodynamic resistance.

The Free Energy Functional. Let \mathcal{P}_2(\mathcal{K}) be the space of probability measures on \mathcal{K} with finite second moment, equipped with the 2‑Wasserstein distance W_2. The instantaneous knowledge distribution \mu \in \mathcal{P}_2(\mathcal{K}) evolves under the free energy functional

\mathcal{F}(\mu) = \beta \underbrace{\int \mu(x) \log \mu(x) dx}_{\text{Entropy } \mathcal{S}(\mu)} - \underbrace{\left( \int V(x)\mu(x)dx + \frac{1}{2}\iint W(x,y)\mu(x)\mu(y)dxdy \right)}_{\text{Utility } \mathcal{U}(\mu)},

where:

· \beta > 0 is the energy‑awareness parameter (inverse of available energy),
· V(x) is the standalone semantic value of item x,
· W(x,y) captures synergistic connectivity between items.

Wasserstein Gradient Flow. The steepest descent of \mathcal{F} in the Wasserstein metric yields the nonlinear Fokker‑Planck equation:

\frac{\partial \mu_t}{\partial t} = \nabla \cdot \left( \mu_t \nabla \frac{\delta \mathcal{F}}{\delta \mu_t} \right)
= -\nabla \cdot \big( \mu_t \nabla (V + W * \mu_t) \big) + \beta \Delta \mu_t,

where W * \mu_t = \int W(\cdot, y) \mu_t(y) dy. This is a drift‑diffusion process: the drift concentrates mass on high‑utility regions, while diffusion (\beta \Delta \mu_t) models forgetting and entropic spreading.

Lyapunov Function and Exponential Convergence. Let \mu^* be the unique minimizer of \mathcal{F} (the minimal survivable knowledge core). Define the Lyapunov candidate

\mathbf{V}(\mu_t) = \mathcal{F}(\mu_t) - \mathcal{F}(\mu^*).

Theorem 2.7 (Lyapunov Stability of the Knowledge Core). Assume \mathcal{F} is \lambda-convex along generalized geodesics in the Wasserstein space. Then:

1. \mathbf{V}(\mu_t) \ge 0, with equality iff \mu_t = \mu^*;
2. Along the gradient flow, \frac{d}{dt} \mathbf{V}(\mu_t) = -\mathcal{I}_{\mathcal{F}}(\mu_t) \le 0, where \mathcal{I}_{\mathcal{F}} is the generalized Fisher information;
3. If a logarithmic Sobolev inequality holds, then \mathbf{V}(\mu_t) \le \mathbf{V}(\mu_0) e^{-2\lambda t} and W_2(\mu_t, \mu^*) \le \sqrt{\frac{2\mathbf{V}(\mu_0)}{\lambda}} e^{-\lambda t}.

Proof. (1) follows from \mu^* being the global minimum. (2) Compute the time derivative:

\frac{d}{dt} \mathcal{F}(\mu_t) = \int \frac{\delta \mathcal{F}}{\delta \mu_t} \partial_t \mu_t = -\int \mu_t \left| \nabla \frac{\delta \mathcal{F}}{\delta \mu_t} \right|^2 = -\mathcal{I}_{\mathcal{F}}(\mu_t) \le 0.

(3) The assumed log‑Sobolev inequality states \mathcal{I}_{\mathcal{F}}(\mu) \ge 2\lambda \mathbf{V}(\mu). Hence \frac{d}{dt} \mathbf{V} \le -2\lambda \mathbf{V}, leading to exponential decay by Grönwall’s lemma. The Wasserstein bound follows from the HWI inequality. ∎

This proof structurally guarantees that when the colony is forced to shed cognitive load due to energy scarcity (a spike in \beta), the pruning process does not cause chaotic forgetting. Instead, the distribution converges exponentially to \mu^*, the essential core. Stochastic perturbations from radiation corruption (\delta_K) are counteracted by this strong Lyapunov stability, constantly pulling the system back into the basin of attraction.

