Chapter 3 – Human–AI Cognitive Contract: Bayesian Game‑Theoretic Formalisation

Knowledge Decision Game. In each round, a task t arrives. Both human collective H and AI memory system M propose responses r_H, r_M. The true value v \in \{0,1\}. Payoffs are given by a matrix L(a_H, a_M; v) penalising errors differently by criticality \kappa. The AI receives a noisy signal with precision \alpha; the human has a private, context‑rich signal with precision \gamma. Utility functions incorporate both accuracy costs and communication delay costs for the human.

Proposition 3.1 (Existence of a Handover PBE). There exists a Perfect Bayesian Equilibrium with a threshold \kappa^* such that tasks with \kappa > \kappa^* are decided by the human, otherwise by the AI. The threshold satisfies the indifference condition

\mathbb{E}[ \text{Cost}_{\text{delay}} + \text{Loss}_{\text{error}} \mid \kappa = \kappa^*, \text{human} ] = \mathbb{E}[ \text{Loss}_{\text{error}} \mid \kappa = \kappa^*, \text{AI} ].

Proof sketch. Construct a strategy profile where the AI’s recommendation is accepted if \kappa \le \kappa^* and ignored otherwise; the human incurs a fixed delay cost but provides perfect accuracy if consulted. By backward induction, the threshold is determined by equating the expected marginal costs. The equilibrium is supported by belief updating consistent with Bayes’ rule. Full details are given in Appendix B.

Future experimental plan: A human‑subject study in an analogue habitat (HI‑SEAS or Antarctic) will vary task criticality, measure trust calibration, and fit a structural model to estimate \kappa^* and validate the equilibrium.

