Chapter 4 – Temporal Coherence via Safety‑Weighted CRDTs

Definition 4.1 (Safety‑Weighted CRDT). A knowledge item is modeled as a join‑semilattice (\mathcal{D}, \sqcup). Operations are pairs (o, \kappa) with criticality tag \kappa. The merge of two states d_1, d_2 is

d_1 \sqcup_\kappa d_2 = 
\begin{cases}
d_1 \sqcup d_2 & \text{if } \max(\kappa(d_1), \kappa(d_2)) \le \kappa_{\text{safe}}, \\
\text{escalate}(d_1, d_2) & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

Theorem 4.2 (Safety Preservation and Commutativity).

1. If the predicate “safety constraints hold” is downward‑closed, then \sqcup_\kappa preserves safety.
2. \sqcup_\kappa is commutative, associative, and idempotent on the low‑criticality subset.

Proof. (1) Downward closure ensures that the join of two safe states remains safe. (2) The standard lattice join \sqcup is commutative, associative, and idempotent. The operator \sqcup_\kappa coincides with \sqcup on the safe subset, inheriting these properties. ∎

Lipschitz Divergence Bound. Define a semantic distance \Delta(d_1, d_2) as the lattice height difference. The merge operator satisfies

\Delta( \sqcup_\kappa(d_1, d_2), d_i ) \le L \cdot \max( \Delta(d_1, d_2), \text{escalation\_cost} ),

with Lipschitz constant L bounded by the maximum change in semantic content. For bounded concurrent updates k and latency \Delta_{\text{latency}}, the maximum divergence between Earth and Moon replicas before escalation is O(k \cdot \Delta_{\text{latency}}).

Future experimental plan: Implement the safety‑weighted CRDT store on a distributed database (e.g., Riak) and stress‑test under simulated lunar network conditions (ns‑3 traces) to measure convergence time and escalation rates.

