Chapter 6 – Research Gaps as Theorems and Conjectures with Proof Sketches

6.1 Gap 1: Radiation‑Resilient Semantic Codes on Ramanujan Graphs

Theorem 6.1 (Expansion‑Driven Error Resilience). Let a knowledge base be encoded as vertex labels of a d-regular Ramanujan expander graph of size n, where semantic distance between items corresponds to graph distance. Then any corruption pattern altering at most w vertices (where w < \frac{n}{2} \cdot \frac{\lambda}{d}, with \lambda the second eigenvalue) leaves the global semantic structure recoverable with fidelity \Phi \ge 1 - O(w/n).

Proof sketch. The expander mixing lemma bounds the edge distribution after corruption. Applying spectral clustering, the corrupted subgraph remains identifiable as a small perturbation of the original. Rate‑resilience trade‑off: using the probabilistic method (Lovász Local Lemma), one can show existence of a labeling scheme achieving w = \Omega(n) with high probability, yielding constant‑rate coding with bounded fidelity loss. Full details in Appendix C.

Future experimental plan: Monte Carlo simulation with particle‑transport‑derived error traces (SPENVIS) will test encoded data on expander topologies versus Reed‑Solomon baselines.

6.2 Gap 2: Tacit Knowledge Formalisation

Theorem 6.2 (Apprenticeship Learning Convergence). Let the expert policy \pi_E be stationary and the AI uses a behavioral cloning algorithm with a hypothesis class of bounded complexity. After observing m state‑action pairs, with probability \ge 1-\delta,

\|\pi_{AI} - \pi_E\|_\infty \le O\left( \sqrt{\frac{\log(1/\delta)}{m}} \right).

Proof sketch. Apply McDiarmid’s inequality to the empirical risk minimization. The bounded difference condition holds because the policy class has finite VC‑dimension. The bound improves to exponential under strong convexity assumptions on the imitation loss.

Future experimental plan: An AI observer (wearable cameras, interaction logs) will shadow life‑support technicians during an overwinter Antarctic season, building a behavioral model. Post‑season, the model will be tested on simulated fault scenarios; its recommendations compared with expert retrospective think‑aloud.

6.3 Gap 3: Safety‑Weighted CRDT Merge — Lipschitz Bound

Theorem 6.3 (Bounded Semantic Divergence). For a safety‑weighted merge operator on a lattice of bounded height H, under a maximum of k concurrent updates and communication latency \Delta, the maximum semantic divergence \delta before escalation satisfies

\delta \le k \cdot \Delta \cdot L,

where L is the Lipschitz constant of the merge with respect to the edit distance.

Proof. The merge operator restricted to safe states is the lattice join, which is 1‑Lipschitz. The escalation mechanism caps divergence at the threshold \kappa_{\text{safe}}. By induction on the update sequence, the bound holds.

6.4 Gap 4: Meaning Preservation via Bisimulation Invariants

Conjecture 6.4. There exists a family of invariants I_j derived from coalgebraic bisimulation such that I_j(T(k)) = I_j(k) for all k iff transformation T preserves all task‑relevant semantics.

Approach: Define a transition system on knowledge items where actions are queries. Invariants are characteristic modal formulas. Completeness is proved for a fragment of the modal \mu-calculus.

6.5 Gap 5: Civilizational Memory Degradation — Minimum Viable Context Population

Theorem 6.5 (Population‑Dependent Decay and P^*). With population‑dependent decay \lambda_6(P) = \lambda_{6,0} P_{\text{ref}} / P(t), the fidelity dynamics yield a critical population P^* = \lambda_{6,0} P_{\text{ref}} / v_6^{\max} below which \mathrm{MRI}_6 < 1 and steady‑state fidelity falls below 0.5 regardless of regeneration effort.

Proof. From the steady‑state equation F_6^* = v_6 / (\lambda_6(P) + v_6). Setting \mathrm{MRI}_6 = 1 requires v_6 = \lambda_6(P). With maximal effort v_6^{\max}, the minimum viable population solves v_6^{\max} = \lambda_{6,0} P_{\text{ref}} / P^*. ∎

Future validation plan: Historical case‑control study of Apollo telemetry loss, BBC Domesday Project, and early digital art to calibrate \lambda_{6,0}; agent‑based simulation to find percolation threshold n_c in social‑cognitive networks.

