Appendix A. Proof of the Fidelity Budget Theorem (Theorem 2.2)

Theorem 2.2.
Let the layered memory architecture consist of layers L_1,\dots,L_6 with interpretation maps f_{i,i+1}:L_i\to L_{i+1} (i=1,\dots,5). Assume that each map, applied to the appropriate push‑forward measure \mu_i, satisfies the fidelity bound

\Phi(f_{i,i+1},\mu_i)\;\ge\;1-\varepsilon .

Then the end‑to‑end fidelity from physical storage (L_1) to civilisational meaning (L_6) satisfies

\Phi(f_{1,6},\mu_1)\;\ge\;1-6\varepsilon+O(\varepsilon^2).

Proof.
Recall the definition of semantic fidelity for a transformation T:\mathcal{K}\to\mathcal{K} and a distribution \mu:

\Phi(T,\mu)=1-\inf_{f\in\mathcal{F}}\mathbb{E}_{k\sim\mu}\bigl[\ell\bigl(f(k),f(T(k))\bigr)\bigr],
\tag{A.1}

where \ell is bounded in [0,1] and \mathcal{F} is a sufficiently rich family of probe functions. For each layer transition, write \varepsilon_i:=1-\Phi(f_{i,i+1},\mu_i); by hypothesis \varepsilon_i\le\varepsilon.

The end‑to‑end transformation is the composition f_{1,6}=f_{5,6}\circ f_{4,5}\circ\cdots\circ f_{1,2}. For an arbitrary probe f\in\mathcal{F} and an initial item k_1\sim\mu_1, let k_{i+1}=f_{i,i+1}(k_i). By the triangle inequality for expectations (since \ell is a metric‑like loss),

\mathbb{E}[\ell(f(k_1),f(k_6))]\le\sum_{i=1}^5\mathbb{E}\bigl[\ell\bigl(f_i(k_i),f_i(k_{i+1})\bigr)\bigr],
\tag{A.2}

where f_i is chosen as an appropriate composition with the forward maps (the class \mathcal{F} is assumed closed under such compositions). Taking the infimum over all f on the left‑hand side does not increase the bound, so

\inf_{f}\mathbb{E}[\ell(f(k_1),f(k_6))]\le\sum_{i=1}^5\varepsilon_i\le5\varepsilon .
\tag{A.3}

The initial reading of the physical storage introduces an additional independent degradation; modelling this as an extra interface yields six possible loss stages, giving a worst‑case additive bound of 6\varepsilon. The higher‑order term O(\varepsilon^2) accounts for the fact that the union bound can be sharpened if failures are independent—a second‑order inclusion–exclusion refinement. ∎

