Appendix B. Perfect Bayesian Equilibrium of the Knowledge Decision Game (Proposition 3.1)

We formalise the handover problem as a Bayesian game between a Human collective H and an AI memory system M.

Game description.

· A task arrives with publicly known criticality \kappa\in[0,1] and an unknown true state v\in\{0,1\}.
· The AI receives a private signal s_M\in\{0,1\} with accuracy \alpha=\Pr(s_M=v)>1/2.
· The Human receives a private signal s_H with accuracy \gamma>\alpha. If the Human makes the final decision, a delay cost c>0 is incurred.
· Timing: Nature draws v and both signals. The AI publicly announces an action recommendation a_M\in\{0,1\}. The Human then either accepts (a_H=a_M) or overrides and incurs cost c to implement s_H. The final outcome a yields loss \kappa\cdot\mathbf{1}(a\neq v).
· Both players have identical preferences (minimising total expected loss), so the game is cooperative.

Proposition 3.1 (Handover threshold PBE).
There exists a Perfect Bayesian Equilibrium (PBE) characterised by a threshold \kappa^* such that:

· the AI truthfully reports a_M=s_M;
· the Human accepts the recommendation if \kappa\le\kappa^* and overrides if \kappa>\kappa^*.

The threshold is uniquely determined by

\kappa^*=\frac{c}{\gamma-\alpha}.
\tag{B.1}

Proof.
Assume the AI reports truthfully (this will be optimal). After observing a_M, the Human’s posterior probability that the recommendation is correct is \Pr(v=a_M\mid a_M)=\alpha (the signal is independent of \kappa). Hence, if the Human accepts, the expected loss is

L_{\text{accept}}=\kappa(1-\alpha).
\tag{B.2}

If the Human overrides, the delay cost c is incurred and the decision is based on the private signal s_H; the expected error probability is 1-\gamma, yielding

L_{\text{override}}=c+\kappa(1-\gamma).
\tag{B.3}

Accepting is preferable exactly when

\kappa(1-\alpha)\le c+\kappa(1-\gamma)\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\kappa(\gamma-\alpha)\le c.
\tag{B.4}

Hence the Human’s best response is to accept iff \kappa\le\kappa^* with \kappa^*=c/(\gamma-\alpha) (if \gamma\le\alpha the Human would never trust the AI, which is ruled out by assumption).

Given this response, the AI’s recommendation is used only when its error cost is weighted by \kappa\le\kappa^*; for larger \kappa the AI’s recommendation is ignored. Truthful reporting remains optimal because any deviation would only increase the error probability when the recommendation is followed. The belief updating follows Bayes’ rule trivially as the signal precision is independent of \kappa. Thus the strategy profile and beliefs constitute a PBE. ∎

