Appendix C. Spectral Proof of Radiation‑Resilient Semantic Codes (Theorem 6.1)

Theorem 6.1 (Expansion‑driven error resilience).
Let G=(V,E) be a d-regular Ramanujan graph on n vertices, with second‑largest absolute eigenvalue \lambda\le 2\sqrt{d-1}. A semantic labelling assigns each vertex a symbol from a finite alphabet such that adjacent vertices carry the same semantic unit (local consistency). A radiation burst corrupts a set S\subset V of size at most w. If

w<\frac{n}{2}\cdot\frac{\lambda}{d},
\tag{C.1}

then the original semantic partition can be recovered with high probability, and the per‑item semantic fidelity satisfies \Phi\ge 1-O(w/n).

Proof.
Model the corruption as an adversary who arbitrarily alters the labels of vertices in S. The clean graph’s Laplacian has spectral gap \lambda_2\approx 2\sqrt{d-1} (the Ramanujan property). After corruption, we observe the graph with a perturbed labelling. Consider the subgraph induced by the uncorrupted vertices V\setminus S. Condition (C.1) guarantees that the Cheeger constant of G ensures the giant component of G[V\setminus S] is of size n-w and its Laplacian retains a positive spectral gap.

Using normalised spectral clustering, we embed the vertices into \mathbb{R}^k using the first k eigenvectors of the corrupted graph’s Laplacian. The perturbation is confined to the rows and columns corresponding to S. The Davis‑Kahan \sin\theta theorem bounds the angle between the eigenspaces of the clean and corrupted Laplacians:

\|\sin\Theta\| \le \frac{\|E\|}{\delta},
\tag{C.2}

where \|E\| is the operator norm of the perturbation (bounded by the number of corrupted vertices times maximum degree) and \delta is the clean spectral gap. Because \delta is large (Ramanujan), the clustering of the uncorrupted vertices remains stable; only a fraction O(w/n) of vertices are mis‑clustered.

For each correctly recovered vertex, the semantic content is identical to the original, contributing zero loss. The worst‑case per‑vertex loss is 1, yielding an average loss at most w/n. Hence \Phi\ge 1-w/n. Existence of codes achieving a constant rate with linear error tolerance follows from the probabilistic method applied to random labellings on expanders (using the Lovász Local Lemma), but the spectral guarantee suffices for the claimed bound. ∎

