Appendix D. Wasserstein Gradient Flow and Lyapunov Stability of the Knowledge Core (Theorem 2.7)

D.1 Free energy functional

Let \mathcal{P}_2(\mathcal{K}) be the space of probability measures on the knowledge state space \mathcal{K}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^d with finite second moment, equipped with the quadratic Wasserstein distance W_2. The free energy \mathcal{F}:\mathcal{P}_2(\mathcal{K})\to\mathbb{R}\cup\{+\infty\} is defined as

\mathcal{F}(\mu)=\beta\underbrace{\int_{\mathcal{K}}\mu(x)\log\mu(x)\,dx}_{\mathcal{S}(\mu)}\;-\;
\underbrace{\left(\int_{\mathcal{K}} V(x)\mu(x)dx+\frac12\iint_{\mathcal{K}^2} W(x,y)\mu(x)\mu(y)\,dx\,dy\right)}_{\mathcal{U}(\mu)},
\tag{D.1}

with \beta>0 (energy‑awareness parameter), V:\mathcal{K}\to\mathbb{R} a value function, and W:\mathcal{K}^2\to\mathbb{R} a symmetric interaction potential.

D.2 First variation and Wasserstein gradient flow

The first variation of \mathcal{F} at \mu is the unique function \frac{\delta\mathcal{F}}{\delta\mu}:\mathcal{K}\to\mathbb{R} satisfying

\lim_{\varepsilon\to0}\frac{\mathcal{F}(\mu+\varepsilon\chi)}{\varepsilon}= \int\frac{\delta\mathcal{F}}{\delta\mu}(x)\,\chi(dx)

for every signed measure \chi with \int\chi=0. A direct computation yields

\frac{\delta\mathcal{F}}{\delta\mu}(x)=\beta\log\mu(x)+ \beta - V(x) - (W*\mu)(x).
\tag{D.2}

(Here * denotes convolution.)

In the Wasserstein space the steepest descent of \mathcal{F} is the continuity equation

\partial_t\mu_t + \nabla\cdot(\mu_t\mathbf{v}_t)=0,
\qquad
\mathbf{v}_t = -\nabla\frac{\delta\mathcal{F}}{\delta\mu_t}.
\tag{D.3}

Substituting (D.2) gives the nonlinear Fokker–Planck equation

\partial_t\mu_t = \nabla\cdot\Bigl(\mu_t\nabla V + \mu_t\nabla(W*\mu_t)\Bigr) + \beta\Delta\mu_t.
\tag{D.4}

D.3 Lyapunov function and dissipation

Let \mu^* be the unique global minimiser of \mathcal{F} (assuming strict convexity). Define the Lyapunov function

\mathbf{V}(\mu)=\mathcal{F}(\mu)-\mathcal{F}(\mu^*)\ge 0,
\tag{D.5}

with equality iff \mu=\mu^*. Along the gradient flow,

\frac{d}{dt}\mathbf{V}(\mu_t)=\frac{d}{dt}\mathcal{F}(\mu_t)
=\int\frac{\delta\mathcal{F}}{\delta\mu_t}\,\partial_t\mu_t\,dx.
\tag{D.6}

Insert the continuity equation and integrate by parts (assuming sufficient decay at infinity):

\frac{d}{dt}\mathcal{F}(\mu_t)=\int\frac{\delta\mathcal{F}}{\delta\mu_t}
\bigl[-\nabla\cdot(\mu_t\mathbf{v}_t)\bigr]dx
= \int\mu_t\,\mathbf{v}_t\cdot\nabla\frac{\delta\mathcal{F}}{\delta\mu_t}\,dx
= -\int\mu_t\Bigl|\nabla\frac{\delta\mathcal{F}}{\delta\mu_t}\Bigr|^2 dx \le 0.
\tag{D.7}

Define the generalised Fisher information \mathcal{I}_{\mathcal{F}}(\mu)=\int|\nabla\frac{\delta\mathcal{F}}{\delta\mu}|^2 d\mu. Then \frac{d}{dt}\mathbf{V}(\mu_t)=-\mathcal{I}_{\mathcal{F}}(\mu_t)\le 0, and \mathcal{I}_{\mathcal{F}}(\mu)=0 iff \mu=\mu^*.

D.4 Exponential convergence

Assume \mathcal{F} satisfies a logarithmic Sobolev inequality (LSI) with constant \lambda>0:

\mathcal{I}_{\mathcal{F}}(\mu)\ge 2\lambda\bigl(\mathcal{F}(\mu)-\mathcal{F}(\mu^*)\bigr)=2\lambda\mathbf{V}(\mu).
\tag{D.8}

This is equivalent to \lambda-convexity of \mathcal{F} along geodesics in the Wasserstein space (Otto–Villani). Then

\frac{d}{dt}\mathbf{V}(\mu_t)\le -2\lambda\mathbf{V}(\mu_t),

and Grönwall’s lemma gives

\mathbf{V}(\mu_t)\le \mathbf{V}(\mu_0)\,e^{-2\lambda t}.
\tag{D.9}

The HWI inequality relates the Wasserstein distance to the free energy:

\frac{\lambda}{2}W_2(\mu,\mu^*)^2\le \mathbf{V}(\mu).
\tag{D.10}

Hence

W_2(\mu_t,\mu^*)\le \sqrt{\frac{2\mathbf{V}(\mu_0)}{\lambda}}\,e^{-\lambda t},
\tag{D.11}

which completes the proof of exponential convergence to the minimal survivable knowledge core. ∎

