Appendix E. Lipschitz Bound for Safety‑Weighted CRDT Merge (Theorem 6.3)

Theorem 6.3.
Let (\mathcal{D},\sqcup,\le) be a join‑semilattice of bounded height H, endowed with the shortest‑path metric d on its covering graph (the Hasse diagram). The safety‑weighted merge operator is

d_1\sqcup_\kappa d_2=
\begin{cases}
d_1\sqcup d_2, & \max(\kappa(d_1),\kappa(d_2))\le\kappa_{\text{safe}},\\
\text{escalate}(d_1,d_2), & \text{otherwise},
\end{cases}
\tag{E.1}

where “escalate” returns a distinguished conflict state that is frozen until human arbitration. For any two replicas undergoing sequences of concurrent updates of total length at most k during a communication latency \Delta, the maximum semantic divergence before the next synchronisation satisfies

D\le k\cdot L,
\qquad L=1,
\tag{E.2}

where the factor \Delta appears implicitly because the update count k is bounded by the product of the update rate and \Delta.

Proof.
Let the replicas R_E and R_M start from a common state d_0. During a partition of duration \Delta, replica R_M applies a sequence of updates u_1,\dots,u_{k_M} and R_E applies v_1,\dots,v_{k_E}, with k_M+k_E=k. Each update is an operation that moves the state upward in the lattice (monotonic refinement). Because the lattice join is commutative, associative, and idempotent, the state after all local updates is just the join of the initial state with the accumulated updates.

Before reconnection, R_M holds d_M = d_0\sqcup U_M with U_M=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k_M}u_i, and R_E holds d_E = d_0\sqcup U_E similarly. The divergence between them is

D = d(d_M,d_E) = d(d_0\sqcup U_M,\; d_0\sqcup U_E).

In a semilattice the join operation is non‑expansive with respect to the geodesic distance d: for any a,b,c,

d(a\sqcup c,\, b\sqcup c)\le d(a,b).

Hence D\le d(U_M,U_E). The distance between the accumulated updates is at most the sum of the heights of the two sets of updates, which is bounded by the total number of updates k. If each update adds one unit of semantic change, the distance is at most k. The latency \Delta determines how many updates can be generated; with an update rate r, we have k=r\Delta, so D\le r\Delta.

If any update exceeds the criticality threshold, the state freezes, preventing further divergence. In all cases the bound holds with Lipschitz constant L=1. ∎

