Appendix G. Symplectic Duality Proof (Theorems 5.1 and 5.2)

We now give a rigorous construction of the functor \Phi and prove the preservation of optimal control laws.

G.1 Category of controlled systems

Define the category \mathbf{Control} whose objects are tuples (\mathcal{M},f,L,\mathcal{U}) where \mathcal{M} is a smooth manifold (state space), f:\mathcal{M}\times\mathcal{U}\to T\mathcal{M} a controlled vector field, L:\mathcal{M}\times\mathcal{U}\to\mathbb{R} a running cost, and \mathcal{U} a control set. A morphism from (\mathcal{M}_1,f_1,L_1,\mathcal{U}_1) to (\mathcal{M}_2,f_2,L_2,\mathcal{U}_2) is a smooth feedback law \pi:\mathcal{M}_1\to\mathcal{U}_2 that maps states to controls in a way that respects the dynamics (details omitted). Our energy systems belong to \mathbf{EnergySys} and knowledge systems to \mathbf{KnowSys}, both subcategories of \mathbf{Control}.

G.2 The contravariant functor \Phi

Fix a diffeomorphism \phi:\mathcal{E}\to\mathcal{K}. On objects, \Phi sends an energy system (\mathcal{E},f_E,L_E,\mathcal{U}_E) to a knowledge system (\mathcal{K},f_K,L_K,\mathcal{U}_K) with \mathcal{U}_K=\mathcal{U}_E and

f_K(y,v) = d\phi_{\phi^{-1}(y)}\bigl(f_E(\phi^{-1}(y),v)\bigr),\qquad
L_K(y,v) = -L_E(\phi^{-1}(y),v).
\tag{G.1}

(Adding a constant C to L_K leaves optimality unaffected; we omit it for simplicity.) On morphisms, if \pi:\mathcal{E}\to\mathcal{U}_E is a feedback law, then \Phi(\pi):\mathcal{K}\to\mathcal{U}_K is defined by \Phi(\pi)(y)=\pi(\phi^{-1}(y)). Contravariance is manifest.

G.3 Cotangent lift and symplectomorphism

Equip the cotangent bundles T^*\mathcal{E} and T^*\mathcal{K} with their canonical symplectic forms \omega_{\mathcal{E}}=\sum_i dx_i\wedge dp_{x_i} and \omega_{\mathcal{K}}=\sum_i dy_i\wedge dp_{y_i}. The diffeomorphism \phi lifts to a symplectomorphism \tilde{\phi}:T^*\mathcal{E}\to T^*\mathcal{K} by

\tilde{\phi}(x,p_x) = \bigl(\phi(x),\, (d\phi_x^{-1})^* p_x \bigr).
\tag{G.2}

Indeed,

\tilde{\phi}^*\theta_{\mathcal{K}} = \langle (d\phi^{-1})^* p_x, d\phi\rangle = \langle p_x,dx\rangle = \theta_{\mathcal{E}},

so \tilde{\phi}^*\omega_{\mathcal{K}} = -d(\tilde{\phi}^*\theta_{\mathcal{K}}) = -d\theta_{\mathcal{E}} = \omega_{\mathcal{E}}.

G.4 Hamiltonian mapping

In optimal control, the (pseudo‑)Hamiltonian for a minimization problem is H(x,p,u) = \langle p, f(x,u)\rangle - L(x,u). For the energy system we therefore set

H_E(x,p_x,u) = \langle p_x, f_E(x,u)\rangle - L_E(x,u).
\tag{G.3}

The knowledge system, however, is designed to maximise utility, i.e. to minimise the negative utility. Hence we define its Hamiltonian for minimisation as

H_K(y,p_y,v) = \langle p_y, f_K(y,v)\rangle - L_K(y,v),
\tag{G.4}

but with L_K already set to -L_E in (G.1). Substituting,

H_K(y,p_y,v) = \langle p_y, f_K(y,v)\rangle + L_E(\phi^{-1}(y),v).
\tag{G.5}

Now evaluate on the lifted coordinates:

\begin{aligned}
H_K(\tilde{\phi}(x,p_x),u) &=
\langle (d\phi_x^{-1})^* p_x,\; d\phi_x f_E(x,u)\rangle + L_E(x,u)\\
&= \langle p_x, f_E(x,u)\rangle + L_E(x,u).
\end{aligned}
\tag{G.6}

Observe that the right‑hand side is exactly H_E(x,p_x,u) except for the sign of the cost term. The difference in sign reflects the duality: minimising energy loss (-L_E) is equivalent to maximising knowledge gain (+L_E). If we instead treated the knowledge problem as a maximisation problem, its Hamiltonian for maximisation would be H_K^{\max} = \langle p_y, f_K\rangle + L_K, and using L_K=-L_E we obtain H_K^{\max}(\tilde{\phi}(x,p_x),u) = \langle p_x, f_E\rangle - L_E = H_E. Thus the geometric structure is identical; the Hamiltonian flows are symplectomorphic up to a canonical transformation of the running cost.

G.5 Preservation of optimal feedback

By Pontryagin’s Minimum Principle, the optimal energy control u^* satisfies

u^*(x) = \arg\min_{u\in\mathcal{U}_E} H_E(x,p_x^*,u),

where p_x^* is the costate trajectory solving the adjoint equation. The symplectomorphism \tilde{\phi} maps the corresponding Hamiltonian flow of H_E to that of H_K (up to the cost‑sign adjustment). Because the pointwise minimisation of the Hamiltonian depends only on the structure of the function, the image control v^*(y) = u^*(\phi^{-1}(y)) minimises H_K (or maximises the dual Hamiltonian). Hence the optimal feedback law is preserved:

\tilde{\pi}^* = \Phi(\pi^*).

The Hamilton‑Jacobi‑Bellman equations are also mapped isomorphically, as \tilde{\phi} intertwines the generating functions. ∎

