Appendix H. Water‑Filling Proof of Optimal Verification Scheduling (Theorem 2.5)

Theorem 2.5 (Water‑filling policy).
Consider the optimisation problem

\max_{\{x_k\in\{0,1\}\}} \sum_{k} v_k x_k \quad\text{s.t.}\quad \sum_{k} e_k x_k \le E_{\max},
\tag{H.1}

with v_k = \kappa(k)p_{\text{corr}}(k)\Delta\Phi(k) > 0 and e_k = e_{\text{ver}} > 0 constant for all k.

Relaxation to continuous variables. Let x_k\in[0,1]. The Lagrangian is

\mathcal{L} = \sum_k v_k x_k - \lambda\Bigl(\sum_k e_k x_k - E_{\max}\Bigr)
- \sum_k \mu_k(x_k-1) + \sum_k \nu_k x_k,
\tag{H.2}

with multipliers \lambda\ge 0, \mu_k\ge 0, \nu_k\ge 0. The Karush–Kuhn–Tucker conditions give:

1. Stationarity: v_k - \lambda e_k - \mu_k + \nu_k = 0 for all k.
2. Complementary slackness: \mu_k(x_k-1)=0, \nu_k x_k = 0.
3. Primal feasibility and \lambda,\mu_k,\nu_k\ge 0.

Consider an item with 0 < x_k < 1; then \mu_k=\nu_k=0 and we obtain v_k = \lambda e_k. For items with x_k=1, we have \nu_k=0,\mu_k\ge0 so v_k - \lambda e_k = \mu_k \ge 0, i.e. v_k \ge \lambda e_k. For items with x_k=0, \mu_k=0,\nu_k\ge0 gives v_k - \lambda e_k = -\nu_k \le 0, i.e. v_k \le \lambda e_k. Therefore, the optimal decision is a simple threshold: verify k iff v_k \ge \lambda^*, where \lambda^* = \lambda e_{\text{ver}}. The multiplier \lambda (and thus the water‑level \lambda^*) is chosen so that the budget constraint holds with equality:

\sum_{k: v_k \ge \lambda^*} e_{\text{ver}} = E_{\max}.
\tag{H.3}

Since all e_k are equal, this reduces to counting items whose value exceeds the threshold and setting the threshold accordingly. This is the classic water‑filling prescription. ∎

