Appendix I. Self‑Repair Error Bound (Lemma 2.6)

Lemma 2.6 (Confidence‑bounded escalation).
Suppose a corrupted knowledge item is restored by computing the maximum a posteriori (MAP) estimate

\hat{k} = \arg\max_{x\in\mathcal{K}} P(x\mid\mathcal{C},\text{partial observations}),
\tag{I.1}

where \mathcal{C} is a set of contextual constraints. Let \theta_c\in[0,1] be a fixed confidence threshold. If P(\hat{k}\mid\dots) \ge \theta_c, the system accepts the repair; otherwise it escalates to a human. Then the probability that an automatic repair silently introduces an error is at most 1-\theta_c.

Proof.
A silent error occurs if the accepted hypothesis \hat{k} is not the true value k^*, yet the posterior confidence exceeds \theta_c. By the law of total probability,

\Pr(\text{silent error}) = \Pr(\hat{k}\neq k^* \text{ and } P(\hat{k}\mid\dots)\ge\theta_c).

Since k^* is one of the hypotheses, the posterior mass of any single incorrect hypothesis cannot exceed 1 - P(k^*\mid\dots). Moreover, P(k^*\mid\dots) \le 1. The condition P(\hat{k}\mid\dots)\ge\theta_c implies that the probability mass assigned to all incorrect hypotheses is at least \theta_c only if k^* has mass less than 1-\theta_c. By Markov’s inequality, the probability of such a configuration is at most 1-\theta_c. A tighter bound can be derived by noting that \Pr(\hat{k}\neq k^*)\le 1-\theta_c whenever the acceptance rule is used. ∎

